<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Predicting consecutive football match outcomes using tree diagrams</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>{@team@}</b> plays two consecutive matches in the local football league. In each match, the team independently has a probability of \(P(W) = {@str_W@}\) to win, \(P(D) = {@str_D@}\) to draw, and \(P(L) = {@str_L@}\) to lose.</p>

<p>Construct a two-stage tree diagram where each node splits into three branches (W, D, L).</p>

<p><b>a) </b> Find the probability that the team wins <b>both</b> matches.</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as an exact decimal).</i></p>
<p>\(P(\text{Win and Win}) =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> Find the probability that the team draws <b>both</b> matches.</p>
<p>\(P(\text{Draw and Draw}) =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>c) </b> Find the probability that the team wins <b>at least one</b> match. </p>
<p><i>(Hint: It is easier to use the complement rule. First find the probability of winning NO matches, which means getting a Draw or a Loss in both matches).</i></p>
<p>\(P(\text{At least one Win}) =\) [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>Because the matches are independent, the branch probabilities remain exactly the same at both levels of the tree diagram.</p>

<p><b>Part a: Winning Both</b><br>
To find the probability of a specific sequence, we multiply the probabilities along that path:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(W, W) = P(W) \times P(W) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(W, W) = {@str_W@} \times {@str_W@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(\text{Win both}) = {@ta1_str@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: Drawing Both</b><br>
Similarly, we multiply the draw probabilities:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(D, D) = P(D) \times P(D) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(D, D) = {@str_D@} \times {@str_D@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(\text{Draw both}) = {@ta2_str@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part c: Winning At Least One Match</b><br>
The easiest way to calculate "at least one" is to use the complement of "none". The team wins ZERO matches if they get a Draw or a Loss in both games. The probability of not winning a single match is:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{Not Winning}) = P(D) + P(L) = {@str_D@} + {@str_L@} = {@P_Not_W_str@} \)</p>
<p>The probability of this happening twice in a row is:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{Zero Wins}) = {@P_Not_W_str@} \times {@P_Not_W_str@} = {@P_Zero_Wins_str@} \)</p>
<p>Therefore, the probability of winning <i>at least one</i> match is the complement of winning zero matches:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{At least one Win}) = 1 - P(\text{Zero Wins}) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{At least one Win}) = 1 - {@P_Zero_Wins_str@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(\text{At least one Win}) = {@ta3_str@} } \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
